<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco de Quevedo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1580">1580</a>–<a href="/years/1645">1645</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco de Quevedo was a Spanish poet, satirist and prose writer of the Golden Age. Known for brilliant conceptist wordplay, he wrote the picaresque novel El Buscón, the satirical Sueños and a large body of poetry, and he feuded bitterly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-gongora">Luis de Góngora</a>. He was also active in politics; arrested in 1639 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares">Count-Duke of Olivares</a>, he was imprisoned until 1643.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-quevedo.jpg" alt="Francisco de Quevedo" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Juan van der Hamen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quevedo_(copia_de_Vel%C3%A1zquez).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…de Cervantes’s Don Quixote (1605–1615), the plays of Lope de Vega and Pedro Calderón de la Barca, the poetry of Luis de Góngora and <b>Francisco de Quevedo</b>, and the paintings of El Greco, Diego Velázquez, whose Las Meninas dates from 1656, Francisco de Zurbarán and Bartolomé Esteban Murillo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Villanueva de los Infantes, Ciudad Real, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1622" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4459 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francisco de Quevedo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1622">What was here in 1622? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The death of laughter was the last of all, and surrounded with a throng of people, hasty to believe, and slow to repent, living without fear of justice, and dying without hope of mercy.&#8221;</p><footer>The Visions (1627), the Vision of Death &#183; tr. Roger L'Estrange, 1667 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/41908/pg41908-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I told her, that I had seen it in the corruption and avarice of wicked magistrates; in the pride and haughtiness of grandees; in the appetites of the voluptuous…&#8221;</p><footer>The Visions (1627), the Vision of Death &#183; tr. Roger L'Estrange, 1667 &#183; the narrator, telling Death where he had seen hell before &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/41908/pg41908-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
